{
  "reconstructed_theorem_text": "For every natural number n, including n = 0, and every signed real-valued function f on the coordinate space Fin n → ℝ that is twice continuously Fréchet differentiable and has compact support, let γ be the product of n centered scalar Gaussian probability laws of variance one, and let D_i f(x) be the real Fréchet derivative of f at x applied to the coordinate vector with entry 1 at i and 0 elsewhere. The functions f², f² log(f²), every (D_i f)², and the finite coordinate sum Σ_i (D_i f)² are integrable with respect to γ. Writing A = ∫ f² dγ, one has ∫ f² log(f²) dγ − A log A ≤ 2 ∫ Σ_i (D_i f)² dγ. The logarithm is Lean Real.log, defined also at zero with log 0 = 0. No assumption A = 1 or positivity of f or A is imposed. At n = 0 the carrier is a singleton, γ is its probability law, the coordinate sum is empty and equals zero, and the entropy difference is zero. The zero function and zero square mass are included. The energy is the coordinate square sum, not the squared operator norm of the derivative for the default Pi supremum norm.",
  "objects": "An arbitrary n ∈ ℕ; a signed function f : (Fin n → ℝ) → ℝ; coordinate directions e_i = Pi.single i 1 for i ∈ Fin n; derivatives D_i f(x) = fderiv ℝ f x e_i; the square mass A = ∫ f(x)² dγ.",
  "assumptions": "Only ContDiff ℝ 2 f and HasCompactSupport f: f is twice continuously real Fréchet differentiable and the closure of its nonzero support is compact. There are no public positivity, normalization, integrability, inequality, or limit-certificate premises.",
  "ambient_space": "The literal finite real coordinate function space Fin n → ℝ, with its default Pi topology, supremum norm and Borel measurable structure. n ranges over all natural numbers, with no positive-dimension restriction. For n = 0 this is the singleton empty-coordinate space.",
  "measures": "γ = Measure.pi (fun _ : Fin n => gaussianReal 0 1), the actual product probability measure of independent centered scalar Gaussian laws with variance one. For n = 0 it is the probability law of the singleton. Every displayed integral is a real Bochner integral with respect to γ.",
  "operators": "fderiv ℝ f x is the real Fréchet derivative. It is evaluated at each literal coordinate direction Pi.single i 1. The energy is Σ_{i ∈ Fin n} (D_i f(x))², an empty sum equal to zero when n = 0; it is not norm(fderiv ℝ f x)² for the default Pi supremum norm. Real.log is totalized and Real.log 0 = 0.",
  "conclusion": "Genuine L1 integrability under γ holds for x ↦ f(x)², x ↦ f(x)² Real.log(f(x)²), each x ↦ (D_i f(x))², and x ↦ Σ_i (D_i f(x))². In addition, ∫ f² Real.log(f²) dγ − (∫ f² dγ) Real.log(∫ f² dγ) ≤ 2 ∫ Σ_i (D_i f)² dγ. The constant 2 is independent of n and f.",
  "scope_boundary": "The statement covers only the specified compactly supported C² signed real functions and the literal variance-one Gaussian product measure. It asserts the displayed homogeneous entropy bound without unit-square-mass normalization. It includes f = 0 and A = 0 via the totalized logarithm. At n = 0 the left side and energy are both zero. It does not assert extension to other function classes, other measures, other derivative norms, or any proof/source-equivalence admission.",
